WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.770
Billy Madison: I leave
the hair silky and smooth!

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.100
Oh, really, fool?

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.650
Really!

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.093
Narrator: There's a reason why

00:00:08.093 --> 00:00:10.390
some people find this funny.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.960
Billy: Stop looking at me, swan!

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.493
Narrator: And the answer lies

00:00:14.493 --> 00:00:16.813
in the genius of Adam Sandler.

00:00:22.928 --> 00:00:24.220
If you're a fan of comedy,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.528
chances are you've seen

00:00:25.528 --> 00:00:28.352
at least one Adam Sandler movie.

00:00:28.352 --> 00:00:31.660
And it's not just because
he's made a lot of them.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.950
Whether you love him or you hate him,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.580
Sandler has had a profound
impact on the genre.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.340
And until recently, he was
one of the most consistent

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.420
and reliable comedians
working in Hollywood.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.210
His earlier works shaped the '90s

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.830
as the golden age of Hollywood comedy

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:51.200
and helped define the
genre for years to come.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.742
And although it can be said that

00:00:52.742 --> 00:00:56.060
a majority of his films
aren't exactly great,

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.780
it's hard to argue that
Sandler isn't watchable.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.670
In fact, it's what draws us to his films.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.700
Not the story,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.070
but his almost suspiciously
charming performances.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.160
The reason behind his charm, I think,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.100
lies in Sandler's distinct
on-screen persona.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.750
Of course, every actor has a unique way

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.810
in which they portray
themselves on screen,

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.090
but none are more exaggerated
than in the genre of comedy.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.820
From Jim Carrey's physically
goofy performances

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.430
to Bill Murray's dry, nonchalant wit.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.676
Rita: I studied 19th
century French poetry.

00:01:31.676 --> 00:01:34.010
Phil: [laughs] What a waste of time.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.560
Narrator: Comedians rely
on their signature persona

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.420
perhaps more than any other actors.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.290
It's the reason why it's so shocking

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.140
to see them in more serious roles

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.160
that we're not familiar with.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.600
Adam Sandler is no exception.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.500
Over the years, Sandler has played

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.470
almost every conceivable role,

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.620
from the son of Satan to
an ex-military hairstylist,

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.060
but they all share the same personality

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.710
that he has honed for ages:

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.270
the persona of a child.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.840
Sandler's performance as an irresponsible

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.150
and emotionally immature man-child

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.020
has more or less become his trademark.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.480
It's a persona that can be traced

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.850
all the way back to the
beginning of his career

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:17.950
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.410
where he played a famous
recurring character

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.200
known as Canteen Boy, an
innocent and naive scoutmaster

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.574
who often became the target of ridicule.

00:02:26.574 --> 00:02:28.270
Mr. MacEntire: Hey, Canteen Boy,

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.390
shouldn't you ask your mother

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:31.330
before you buy something for the house?

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:32.961
Canteen Boy: Thanks for
asking, Mr. MacEntire.

00:02:32.961 --> 00:02:34.875
Actually, I've been given carte blanche

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:36.868
to furnish my room as I see fit.

00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:38.220
Stan: Wow!

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.800
Narrator: It was this
character, among many others,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.710
that jump-started Sandler's career

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.470
and made him popular
among younger audiences,

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.847
eventually leading him to
his first breakout film,

00:02:47.847 --> 00:02:50.830
"Billy Madison" in 1995,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:53.410
where he played a spoiled, rich man-child

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.720
who had to repeat grades one through 12

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.600
to prove his maturity and
inherit his father's business.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.538
In a lot of ways, "Billy Madison"

00:03:01.538 --> 00:03:04.250
was a film made for Adam Sandler,

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.370
written by himself and
screenwriter Tim Herlihy,

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:11.500
whom Sandler had collaborated
with in his years at "SNL."

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.680
The two understood the unique charm

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.160
and humor of the childlike persona

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.690
that Sandler was great at portraying.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.810
And it was their constant collaboration

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.440
that led to some of Sandler's best works,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.037
such as "Happy Gilmore,"
"The Wedding Singer,"

00:03:26.037 --> 00:03:28.820
"The Waterboy," and "Big Daddy."

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.930
But the genius of Sandler's performance

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.610
is not his man-child persona alone.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.320
After all, he's not the
only modern comedian

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.500
to consistently play
this type of character.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:44.050
It's also his unique ability
of portraying innocence.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:44.930
And to understand it,

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.853
we have to go all the way
back to where comedy began.

00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:52.010
The history of using a childlike
persona for comedic effect

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.346
is as long as the history
of comedy in film.

00:03:55.346 --> 00:03:57.210
The king of comedy, Charlie Chaplin,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.950
understood that a
certain amount of empathy

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.000
was necessary to achieve good comedy.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.220
It's why his films often
dealt with real-world problems

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.220
and issues relevant to his time.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.210
Chaplin understood that while laughing

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:14.250
at someone experiencing
a series of misfortune

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:18.170
might be sadistic, there is
comedy in watching it happen

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:19.870
to someone who doesn't quite see

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.693
or comprehend it as tragedy.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.220
This is what made his persona of The Tramp

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.860
so popular and timeless.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.930
It showed that tragedy can
be interpreted as comedy,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.320
depending on the angle
from which we view it.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:35.500
It's the same philosophy

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.870
that makes Sandler's
performances so charming.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.510
The ridiculous situations
that Sandler's characters

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.510
find themselves in more often than not

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.987
stem from real-life problems,

00:04:45.987 --> 00:04:49.513
like his grandmother's eviction
notice in "Happy Gilmore"

00:04:49.513 --> 00:04:52.580
or the abandonment of
a child in "Big Daddy,"

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.920
which, by the way, was clearly inspired

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.890
by Chaplin's debut feature, "The Kid."

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:00.040
But the characters Sandler portrays

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.330
never see these issues as misfortunes,

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.210
simply because they're unable

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:07.500
to comprehend them the way we do.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.950
Happy Gilmore: But she's an
old lady. I mean, look at her.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.840
She's old.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.440
Narrator: In all his films,
Sandler is essentially

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.991
a child thrown into an adult's world

00:05:16.991 --> 00:05:18.749
that he cannot understand.

00:05:18.749 --> 00:05:22.010
Nor do the real adults
try to understand him,

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.120
a force of innocence
that refuses to change

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.940
despite the obstacles he faces.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.000
Interestingly enough, it's
the reason why children

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.600
are featured so heavily in his films.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.110
In fact, quite often, children are really

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.290
the only ones who understand him,

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:42.280
and it's around children
where he feels most at home.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:45.660
There are a lot of ways
Sandler portrays innocence,

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.760
but I think it's best
exemplified in moments like this.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.270
Robbie: Well, I have a
microphone, and you don't.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:58.270
So you will listen to every
d--- word I have to say!

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:01.210
Narrator: These almost
random, violent outbursts

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:05.310
are, I think, what makes
Sandler's persona so distinct.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.850
Although they seem completely irrational,

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:09.300
the anger that he performs

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.050
is always directed at people or things

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:15.400
that he finds unreasonable and unjust.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.020
More often than not, it's not anger,

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.310
but frustration at his
inability to understand

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:23.990
and accept the realities around him.

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.940
I think it's moments like these

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.140
that make critics describe
his performances as earnest.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:33.217
Playing a childlike character
disables any filters

00:06:33.217 --> 00:06:36.280
on his emotions, words, and actions,

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:40.340
and we see the characters for
who they are, warts and all.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.880
And although comedy is Sandler's forte,

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.010
it's this quality that makes him great

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:46.750
in more dramatic films

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:49.080
like the highly underrated "Reign Over Me"

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.350
and Paul Thomas Anderson's
"Punch-Drunk Love,"

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.680
which many still regard
as Sandler's best work.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.560
PTA understood Sandler's unique ability

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.510
to capture this innocence
and created a work

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:06.440
that showcased a sense of
vulnerability that came with it.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:09.885
Barry: I don't like myself sometimes.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.670
Can you help me?

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:16.750
Narrator: Once you begin to understand

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.630
the genius of Adam Sandler,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:20.996
it's easy to see why his career

00:07:20.996 --> 00:07:22.854
turned out the way it did.

00:07:22.854 --> 00:07:24.190
The biggest problem, I think,

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:26.070
stems from the fact that Sandler

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:28.960
has simply outgrown his innocence.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:32.440
His films portray the same real
problems that they once did.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:33.898
Jack: We can't lose Dunkin' Donuts,

00:07:33.898 --> 00:07:35.764
they're our biggest client!

00:07:36.864 --> 00:07:38.280
We're gonna go bankrupt, buddy.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.750
And we have 200 employees relying on us.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:41.640
Narrator: But it's no longer

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.491
through the lens of a child, but an adult.

00:07:45.491 --> 00:07:48.800
The humor that once originated
from his childlike innocence

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.790
began turning into more strangely

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.130
political and racist jokes.

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.737
Jill: I love how nice we
are to this homeless man,

00:07:55.737 --> 00:07:57.093
a person we don't even know

00:07:57.093 --> 00:07:59.058
who probably is pretending to be homeless!

00:07:59.058 --> 00:08:00.953
You don't look homeless to me! You're fat!

00:08:00.953 --> 00:08:02.350
You're al-Qaida!

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:04.430
Narrator: And without any
of his former innocence,

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:07.852
the outbursts simply come
off as rude and obnoxious.

00:08:07.852 --> 00:08:10.660
Jack: It's not, it's not,
it's not, it's not, all right?

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.460
Jill: All right, then why
are you getting so angry?

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:13.980
Jack: Why are you so annoying?

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:14.920
Narrator: It's even sadder

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.060
because we all know that Sandler

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.910
is capable of putting
on a good performance.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:22.290
His recent works like
"The Meyerowitz Stories"

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:25.180
and "Uncut Gems" are proof of it.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.560
And as a fan, I miss Sandler's
better comedic works,

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.170
the ones that let you sit back

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.680
and simply enjoy with your friends.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:35.130
Because as much as what
differentiates Sandler

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:38.330
might be in his ability
to portray innocence,

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.740
the true genius of Adam Sandler

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:42.530
might be in creating a work

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:45.439
that genuinely wants
you to have a good time.

00:08:45.439 --> 00:08:48.106
[both laughing]

